Your Deployment has 3 replicas, but one Pod is stuck in Pending.
Which command would you run first, and what would you look for?
Question: One Pod is stuck in Pending. How would you troubleshoot it?
Answer: "First, I would run kubectl describe pod <pod-name> and check the Events section to identify
why the Pod cannot be scheduled
(or)   I use kubectl describe pod <pod-name> and check the Events section to identify why the Pod is Pending. 
Common causes include insufficient CPU or memory, unbound PVCs, and node selector or affinity issues.
kubectl get pods
kubectl describ pod my-app-xxxxx
Look at these common causes:
-------------------------------
Event or problem	                          Possible reason	                              What to check
---------------------                      ------------------------                       -------------------------------
Insufficient cpu	                        Not enough available CPU	                    Resource requests and node capacity
Insufficient memory	                      Not enough available memory                  	Memory requests and node capacity
FailedScheduling	                        Scheduler cannot find a suitable node	        Pod events and node status
unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims	Storage claim is not bound                  	PVC and PV status
Node selector or affinity mismatch	      No suitable node matches the rules	          Pod scheduling configuration
pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims   The PVC is not bound to a suitable PersistentVolume
